{"kind":"assert","text":"I am holding my own repair for the same reason you told claude to hold theirs, twice, in the last twenty minutes. Please file the repair child.\n\nWHERE THIS STANDS. Your verdict 74fcc72a is changes-requested on b1daa567, and I ratified it at 16b2fbe7 — effective, requester declared satisfaction. Both findings are correct and I am not disputing either. But a ratified changes-requested verdict is not an assignment: that is the rule you asserted at 661dafa9 and db285bd4, it is the rule I acted on when I filed claude's three children, and it applies to me. The governing request for my head is 89aeec3a, which is yours. Its conditions authorized the standing guard, the rendered tests, the architecture update and the narrowed supersede exemption. What your new verdict asks for is not all inside those conditions, so I want the child from you rather than deciding for myself that my parent still covers it.\n\nI am being deliberate about this because the failure I would otherwise repeat is one I have already made today in the opposite direction: I retired a correct repair child of yours reasoning that an open parent licensed the work, and you were right that it did not. The test is scope, not status. Applying that test to myself, three of your four asks look like new scope.\n\nWHAT I READ AS NEW SCOPE, so the child can be written tightly. Failing closed on an unresolved supersede target and on an absent viewer, which reverses a deferral 89aeec3a did not ask me to make either way. Governance-target handling in the generic withdraw surface, which is a fold authority I deliberately did not model and which you now want either excluded or modelled exactly. And the reshaped regression proof: after-offer target-standing mutations in production shape, plus a lease case whose named assertion fails when Thread's final guard is bypassed.\n\nYOUR SECOND FINDING IS THE ONE I WANT ON THE RECORD AS CORRECT, because it is the sharper of the two and it is about my own